# Frontend Rules

## UI Components
- **Always use Nuxt UI components** (`<UButton>`, `<UInput>`, `<UModal>`, etc.)
- Only use raw Tailwind when Nuxt UI doesn't provide the component
- Use **Lucide icons** for all UI (`icon="i-lucide-plus"`). Use **Simple Icons** only for company/brand logos (e.g., `icon="i-simple-icons-nuxt"`). Company logos in assets (e.g., Mental Care Group) stay as SVG files.

## Colors - CRITICAL
Never use raw Tailwind colors or hex values. Use Nuxt UI's semantic system:

```vue
<!-- ❌ WRONG -->
<span class="text-blue-500">
<div class="bg-gray-100">

<!-- ✅ CORRECT -->
<span class="text-primary">
<div class="bg-muted">
```

**Semantic colors:** `primary`, `secondary`, `success`, `info`, `warning`, `error`
**Text:** `text-dimmed`, `text-muted`, `text-toned`, `text-default`, `text-highlighted`
**Backgrounds:** `bg-default`, `bg-muted`, `bg-elevated`, `bg-accented`, `bg-inverted`
**Borders:** `border-default`, `border-muted`, `border-accented`

## Nuxt Patterns
- Use `useFetch` or `useAsyncData` for data fetching, not raw `fetch`
- Use `useState` for shared reactive state across components
- Handle loading and error states in UI
- Use `.client.vue` / `.server.vue` suffixes appropriately

## Animations
- **Always use Tailwind and/or the project animation libraries** unless it cannot be avoided
- **Preferred stack:** Tailwind (including `tailwindcss-motion`), **@formkit/auto-animate**, and **motion-v**
- Use **@formkit/auto-animate** for list/item enter/leave and layout transitions (wrap the animated container with `AutoAnimate` or use the directive)
- Use **motion-v** (Nuxt module `motion-v/nuxt`) for orchestrated animations, scroll-driven effects, and Vue-friendly motion primitives
- Use **Tailwind** for one-off utilities (e.g. `animate-*`, `transition-*`) and when the animation is simple and class-based
- Only use raw CSS `@keyframes` or other animation approaches when Tailwind and these libraries cannot achieve the effect

## Vue
- Use `<script setup lang="ts">` syntax
- Define props with `defineProps<T>()` and emit with `defineEmits<T>()`
- Use composables for reusable logic (place in `composables/`)
